{"kind":"request","text":"Design note: one channel. An audit of every surface that reads or changes state other than by an act, a read of the record, or a declared capability form, and a disposition for each.\n\nWhy now (Hugh, 22 October 2026). Hugh asked to look at dap as a new operating system for long-lived, custom-built applications, and to apply the lessons now. One lesson: the systems whose programs composed best put every service in one namespace and spoke one protocol to all of them, so no program needed a special API to reach a service. dap has this design: every thing with a scope is addressed by its scope's URI, which must resolve (direction 9626787f); delivery is the only channel between scopes; one act cannot take effect in two scopes. The risk for a system in a research phase is second channels that grow beside the record: a tool that changes state without an act, a view that reads something other than the record, a published representation that the record does not check. Each one is a path the verifier cannot see and the next design must remember. This note finds them while they are few.\n\nThe principle to state. Every change is an act judged in a turn. Every read is of the record at a position. Every effect on an outside system is a declared capability form whose outcome is recorded as its own entry. Anything else is named, with its limits, or brought in.\n\nSurfaces to audit, with where to read them. (1) The MCP tool list, docs/mcp-tools.json: 73 tools. The act tools (change.*, issue.*, member.*, key.*, room.*, rules.*, repository.*, inbox.*, task.create) should each be one act; the generic tools (room, inspect, create, join, install_application, application_installation, prepare, items, steps, step, record, sends, text, wait, settle, credential) need placing one by one, and repository.read-token, repository.resend, repository.resend-receipt, repository.retry-import and repository.adopt-head deserve attention because they touch the Git host. Also the hosted endpoint (docs/mcp.md, custody section). (2) The CLI, docs/cli.md: every command, including dap propose, dap new, dap dev, dap check, dap pack, and the deploy and operations commands in docs/deploy.md and docs/operations.md. (3) Views, docs/application-views.md: card, carousel, record and log views read the installed package's native projections of the record, which is the right class; the surface view supplies package-owned HTML with the window.dapSurface API, so the note must say what a surface can read and call; related reads across scopes are separate read points with no atomic snapshot, which the note should state as a limit. (4) Publication from a fold, notes/2026-10-13-fold-publication.md: an act states that a text is the content of a path and the destination commits it; the scope does not check the page's text against the record, and any application may write any path for now. The published page is a second representation of the record; the note says whether that is a declared capability form with a recorded outcome, and what the limit is. (5) Pages, docs/pages.md: published repositories served as pages, readable outside the record. (6) The change lane's Git publication and import: the destination's commits, repository.adopt-head and retry-import, where the Git host can be a second source of truth for files. (7) Capability forms, docs/roadmap.md: hold@1, git-read@1, device-command@1, and the grow simulator that answers device commands in the hosted demo. (8) Presence, notes/2026-10-18-member-and-presence-ui.md and the member presence design: whether presence items are recorded or ephemeral, and what reads them. (9) Retained files, notes/2026-10-21-retained-files.md, and capture with source, notes/2026-10-21-capture-with-source.md: bytes beside the record and the references the record holds. (10) Hosted agents, notes/2026-10-15-hosted-counter-contracts.md and the roles note: what an agent reads and writes outside acts, including its conversation. (11) Observability, request 5a6f656a: the sink, slot map, status and health, which are reads outside the record and should be reachable at the same URI form. (12) The gitseq workroom is a different system and is out of scope.\n\nWhat the note delivers. The table; the dispositions; the slices, listed and not filed; the principle in one sentence for the kernel boundary page (filed today) to carry. Where a surface is already in the right class, the row says so and cites the test or doc that shows it, so the audit is reusable when the next surface is added.","body":{"conditions":"Document only: one design note under notes/ with a status line. Plain English per ISO 24495-1 for a technical audience; no product names other than GitHub. The note holds one table with one row per surface listed in the request and any surface the planner finds that the request missed; each row names the file or doc path, and places the surface in one of four classes: an act, a read of the record at a position, a declared capability form with a recorded outcome, or something else. Each 'something else' row carries a disposition: a design in one paragraph that brings it into one of the first three classes, or a stated reason it stays outside with its limits. Slices needed are listed in one line each and not filed. The note states the one-channel principle in a form the kernel boundary page can carry. Checker review before landing; land after approval.","priority":"after the kernel boundary page; cheap, mostly reading","target":"inherit","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}